/// The same plural leaf, from a module that DOES import one of its declarers.
///
/// This is the control that separates "the resolver has no scope" from "this
/// one module had none": `Omega` is the LOSER of the leaf index's name-order
/// pick, so binding it here cannot be the arbitrary answer arriving at the
/// right destination by luck.
///
/// The bare leaf under test is the RETURN ANNOTATION. The struct literal in the
/// body is written qualified on purpose, and not to make the test pass: a bare
/// `Widget<T> { .. }` there binds `Alpha::Widget` while the annotation on the
/// same function binds `Omega::Widget`, so the function fails E0200 against
/// itself. A body's bare struct literal does not take the scoped path the
/// annotation takes. That defect is recorded in docs/name-resolution.md §8.2s;
/// leaving it bare here would make this control fail for a reason that has
/// nothing to do with what it controls.
![config(testing)]
namespace ResolutionLeafIndex::Importer;

import ResolutionLeafIndex::Omega;

type struct Packer {
    static pack<T>(v: T) -> Widget<T> {
        return ResolutionLeafIndex::Omega::Widget<T> { v: v };
    }
}
